---
import Guide from '../layouts/Guide.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import PracticeNext from '../components/PracticeNext.astro';
import {installFaqs} from '../data/compare';
import {reaRelease} from '../data/rea-release';
const toc=[{id:'before',label:'Before you start'},{id:'connect',label:'Connect your agent'},{id:'verify',label:'Check the connection'},{id:'engines',label:'Choose an analysis engine'},{id:'troubleshooting',label:'If something goes wrong'},{id:'update',label:'Keep REA up to date'},{id:'faq',label:'Install FAQ'}];
---
<Guide eyebrow="01 / Installation" minutes="6 min" toc={toc} faq={installFaqs}>
  <div class="callout"><p><strong>The short version</strong></p><p>Install a supported Node.js release, run REA setup, review the proposed changes, and restart your coding agent. Then ask it to inspect one small, local target.</p></div>
  <h2 id="before">01. Before you start</h2>
  <p>You need a terminal, npm, and a coding agent with local MCP support. This guide covers Claude Code, Codex and Cursor. Use software you own or have permission to inspect.</p>
  <div class="client-chooser" role="group" aria-label="Choose your operating system"><button aria-pressed="false" data-os="windows">Windows / PowerShell</button><button aria-pressed="true" data-os="unix">macOS / Linux</button></div>
  <div data-windows-help hidden>
    <h3>Windows: start in PowerShell</h3>
    <p>Install a supported Node.js release, then open a new PowerShell window. Use the Windows commands below when your coding agent runs on Windows. If it runs in WSL, install Node.js and REA inside that same WSL distribution and use the Linux commands.</p>
    <p>The .cmd commands avoid PowerShell blocking npm.ps1 or npx.ps1. You do not need to change the execution policy. If node is not found, reopen the terminal after installing Node.js and check that its installation folder is on PATH.</p>
  </div>
  <CodeBlock trackingId="runtime-check" code={'node --version\nnpm --version'} label="Check your runtime"/>
  <p>REA currently supports <strong>Node.js 22.19+ within 22.x, 24.11+ within 24.x, or 26+</strong>. Versions 23, 25 and prereleases are outside its supported range. Get a compatible release from <a href="https://nodejs.org/en/download">Node.js</a> if needed.</p>
  <p>{`Our example checks were run on macOS arm64 with Node.js 22.23.1 and REA ${reaRelease.version}. Platform support for additional engines varies; see the`} <a href="https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/main/docs/installation.md">official installation reference</a> for your target.</p>
  <h2 id="connect">02. Connect your coding agent</h2>
  <p>Choose your client to get a scoped setup command. “Choose in setup” opens the interactive client selection.</p>
  <div class="client-chooser" role="group" aria-label="Choose your coding agent"><button aria-pressed="true" data-client="">Choose in setup</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-client="claude_code">Claude Code</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-client="codex">Codex</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-client="cursor">Cursor</button></div>
  <div id="setup-command"><CodeBlock trackingId="setup-command" code="npx rea-agents@latest setup" label="Run in your terminal"/></div>
  <ol><li>Run the command. npm may first ask to download the package.</li><li>Review REA’s own plan: client configuration files, matching skill instructions, and any optional engine setup.</li><li>Approve the changes you want. If your first target is JavaScript, you can start without a native engine.</li><li><strong>Restart or reconnect your coding agent</strong> so it loads the new MCP server.</li></ol>
  <p>The setup installs both the MCP registration and the matching workflow instructions. Installing only a skill does not establish the tool connection.</p>
  <h3>Prefer to ask your agent?</h3>
  <CodeBlock trackingId="setup-prompt" prompt label="Copy into your coding agent" code="Install REA for this coding agent using npx rea-agents@latest setup. Show me the proposed configuration changes before applying them. Then tell me how to restart and verify the connection. My first target will be a local JavaScript package."/>
  <h2 id="verify">03. Check the connection</h2>
  <p>After restarting, ask the agent whether it can see REA’s tools. For our first tutorial, it should be able to call <code>analyze_javascript_application</code>.</p>
  <CodeBlock trackingId="connection-check" prompt label="Connection check" code="Check whether REA's analyze_javascript_application tool is available in this session. If it is, tell me the local path it needs. If it is missing, report that rather than pretending to run an analysis."/>
  <p>You can also check a client’s configuration from the terminal. Replace <code>codex</code> with <code>claude_code</code> or <code>cursor</code> as appropriate.</p>
  <CodeBlock trackingId="doctor-command" code="npx -y rea-agents@latest doctor --client codex --json"/>
  <div class="callout"><p><strong>Configuration health and a live connection are different.</strong> Doctor checks registration and prerequisites. A successful tool call from your restarted agent is the useful end-to-end check.</p></div>
  <p data-windows-help hidden>Keep setup, doctor and your coding agent in the same environment. A Windows registration does not connect an agent running in WSL. After setup, fully close and reopen the selected client, then run the connection check above.</p>
  <PracticeNext source="install-connection-section"/>
  <h2 id="engines">04. Add the tools your target needs</h2>
  <p>Start with the type of software you want to inspect. You do not need every analysis engine installed.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Target</th><th>What to prepare</th><th>Start here</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>JavaScript / Electron</td><td>Node.js, npm and local package files</td><td><a href="/first-analysis/">First analysis</a></td></tr><tr><td>Native binary</td><td>A supported Ghidra, Hopper or IDA installation</td><td><a href="https://rea.tools/guides/native/">Official native guide</a></td></tr><tr><td>Android APK</td><td>JDK and the headless JADX bridge required by REA</td><td><a href="https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/main/docs/android-analysis.md">Official Android reference</a></td></tr><tr><td>Browser behavior</td><td>A Chrome-family browser and an explicitly configured debugging connection</td><td><a href="https://rea.tools/guides/browser/">Official browser guide</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  <p>Commercial engines have their own licenses. REA does not supply an engine license. Check the official platform requirements before adding one.</p>
  <h2 id="troubleshooting">05. If something goes wrong</h2>
  <h3>The agent cannot see REA</h3><p>Restart the client first. If the tools are still missing, run the scoped doctor command above. You can inspect a setup repair plan without applying it:</p>
  <CodeBlock trackingId="repair-plan" code="npx rea-agents@latest setup --client codex --dry-run --json"/>
  <p>Review the plan, then run the same setup command without <code>--dry-run --json</code> if you want to apply it. Restart again after configuration changes.</p>
  <h3>“rea: command not found”</h3><p>A project-local npm installation does not add <code>rea</code> to your global shell path. The <code>npx</code> commands in this guide work without a global install. If you prefer a permanent CLI:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={'npm install --global rea-agents\nrea --version'}/>
  <h3>An engine check fails</h3><p>A missing native engine does not prevent unrelated static JavaScript analysis. Check the provider required by your specific target. For large native binaries, consult the official engine-specific startup timeout guidance.</p>
  <h2 id="update">06. Keep the client registration current</h2>
  <p>For an npx-based setup, rerun the latest setup, review the changes, and restart the client:</p>
  <CodeBlock code="npx rea-agents@latest setup"/>
  <p>For a global CLI, run <code>rea update</code>, then follow its setup-refresh instructions. A running MCP server will not refresh merely because newer instructions exist on GitHub.</p>
  <a class="next-guide" href="/first-analysis/"><span>Next / Beginner tutorial</span><strong>Find where an app saves exported files. <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></strong></a>
  <h2 id="faq">Install FAQ</h2>
  {installFaqs.map(item => <section class="faq-item"><h3>{item.question}</h3><p>{item.answer}</p></section>)}
  <p><a href="/compare/">Compare REA with GhidraMCP and other tools</a></p>
  <div class="sources"><p>Sources: <a href="https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/main/docs/installation.md">REA installation reference</a>, <a href="https://github.com/morluto/rea">official README</a>, <a href="https://rea.tools/get-started">official get-started page</a>{`, and the locally checked REA ${reaRelease.version} CLI help. Reviewed ${reaRelease.lastVerifiedLabel}. Upstream main may describe features newer than the npm release.`}</p></div>
</Guide>
<script>
  import {track} from '../lib/analytics';
  const clients=[...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-client]')];
  const systems=[...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-os]')];
  const blocks=[...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.code-block:not(.prompt-block)')].map(block=>({block, original:block.querySelector('code')!.textContent!}));
  let client='';
  let windows=false;
  function updateCommands(){
    for(const {block,original} of blocks){
      let command=block.closest('#setup-command')?'npx rea-agents@latest setup'+(client?' --client '+client:''):original.replace(/--client codex/g,'--client '+(client||'codex'));
      if(windows) command=command.replace(/(^|\n)(npx|npm|rea) /g,'$1$2.cmd ');
      block.querySelector('code')!.textContent=command;
      block.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-copy]')!.dataset.copy=command;
    }
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-windows-help]').forEach(panel=>panel.hidden=!windows);
  }
  clients.forEach(button=>button.addEventListener('click',()=>{
    clients.forEach(b=>b.setAttribute('aria-pressed',String(b===button)));
    client=button.dataset.client||'';
    updateCommands();
    track('client_select',{item:client||'interactive'});
  }));
  systems.forEach(button=>button.addEventListener('click',()=>{
    systems.forEach(b=>b.setAttribute('aria-pressed',String(b===button)));
    windows=button.dataset.os==='windows';
    updateCommands();
    track('os_select',{item:button.dataset.os!});
  }));
</script>
